Looking at this problem, I need to determine for which $p \in \mathbb{R}$ the integral $\int_{\mathbb{R}^n} |\log|x||^p\, dx$ converges.

## Proof

**Reduction to a one-dimensional integral.** Using polar coordinates $x = r\theta$ with $r = |x| \geq 0$, $\theta \in S^{n-1}$, and $dx = r^{n-1}\,dr\,d\sigma(\theta)$:

$$\int_{\mathbb{R}^n} |\log|x||^p\, dx = \omega_{n-1}\int_0^\infty |\log r|^p\, r^{n-1}\, dr,$$

where $\omega_{n-1} = |S^{n-1}| > 0$ is the surface area of the unit sphere. The convergence of the original integral is equivalent to the convergence of

$$I(p) = \int_0^\infty |\log r|^p\, r^{n-1}\, dr.$$

We split into three regions: $I_1 = \int_0^{1/2}$, $I_2 = \int_{1/2}^{2}$, $I_3 = \int_2^{\infty}$.

---

**Region $I_3 = \int_2^{\infty} (\log r)^p\, r^{n-1}\, dr$ (near infinity).** Here $\log r > 0$ so $|\log r| = \log r$.

*Case $p \geq 0$:* For $r \geq e$, $(\log r)^p \geq 1$, so
$$I_3 \geq \int_e^{\infty} r^{n-1}\, dr = +\infty \quad (\text{since } n \geq 1).$$

*Case $p < 0$:* Write $q = -p > 0$. The integrand is $\frac{r^{n-1}}{(\log r)^q}$. Since $\log r = o(r^\varepsilon)$ for every $\varepsilon > 0$, for any $q > 0$ and sufficiently large $r$ we have $(\log r)^q \leq r^{(n-1)/2}$ (taking $\varepsilon = (n-1)/(2q)$ when $n \geq 2$). Thus
$$\frac{r^{n-1}}{(\log r)^q} \geq r^{(n-1)/2}, \quad \text{so } I_3 \geq \int_R^{\infty} r^{(n-1)/2}\, dr = +\infty \quad (n \geq 2).$$

For $n = 1$: substitute $r = e^t$, $dr = e^t\,dt$:
$$I_3 = \int_{\log 2}^{\infty} \frac{e^t}{t^q}\, dt = +\infty,$$
since $e^t / t^q \to +\infty$ as $t \to \infty$.

**In every case, $I_3 = +\infty$.**

---

Since $I(p) = I_1 + I_2 + I_3$ and $I_3 = +\infty$ for **every** $p \in \mathbb{R}$ and every $n \geq 1$, the integral $I(p)$ diverges for every $p$.

(For completeness: $I_1$ converges for all $p$ — substituting $r = e^{-t}$ gives $\int_{\log 2}^{\infty} t^p e^{-nt}\,dt$, which converges by exponential dominance. $I_2$ converges iff $p > -1$, since $|\log r| \sim |r - 1|$ near $r = 1$. But these are moot since $I_3$ already forces divergence.)

---

## Conclusion

The integral $\displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^n} |\log|x||^p\, dx$ **diverges for every $p \in \mathbb{R}$** (and every $n \geq 1$). The divergence at infinity is unavoidable: the volume growth $r^{n-1}$ (combined with the fact that $|\log r|^p$ decays slower than any negative power of $r$) makes the tail integral infinite regardless of $p$.

**There are no values of $p$ for which the integral converges.**

